  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Bullard Mountain</strong> is a 4,226-foot summit in Alaska. The Bullard Mountain Trail covers 4.0 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 470 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 1.57, gaining roughly 1,900 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 735 feet, the route tops out near 1,106 feet.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=58.42469,-134.52971\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">58.42469, -134.52971</a> (tap for driving directions). Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Moderate rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 1.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Amherst Peak, Auke Mountain and Mount Juneau \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
